## GRADING (binding; the ONE table; a shape is graded by it wherever it appears; capability decides, a comparison only finds candidates)
- **G-1 importer differential (commit `949bea7`).** A stored option row, a recorded `panelBlocks` entry, a count or the place where the section ends differs from main on any input = **Blocker** (the commission rules Warning-only: a data decision is not the builder's). Any other issue (code, severity, location, text or count) differs, or an import that succeeded at main fails or blocks at the head = **Major**. The new Warning raised for a cell that is NOT, after the trailing `*` and white space are dropped, ordinal-equal to an option SKU the map holds for a device of THAT sheet and heading-shaped (a false positive), or missing for one that is (a false negative) = **Major**; a different Warning code, sentence or location = **Major**. A difference in time or allocation only = Note with your figures.
- **G-2 surviving mutant** on a check that decides what the guard accepts or refuses (the id key, the supply price guard, the id composition table, the membership scope or exactness of the new Warning, the stored-row invariants of the importer change): **Major**, unless a FORCED RUN or a census shows it unreachable through every input path your census found (then **Minor**, census and run attached). Any other survivor: Minor unless proven equivalent (a mutant that changes no behaviour of any caller, proved by a census and a forced run, is an equivalent and goes in its own list). A survivor on a boundary finer than the ticket's named mutant (for example a price guard narrowed to a value between the committed row's price and zero) = Minor with the interval stated.
- **G-3 test validity.** An existing test removed, skipped, loosened or edited = **Major** (always). A new row whose expectation is read from the code under test (a pin that cannot fail on a wrong code value) = **Major**; a new row green at main's source that the builder says is red (or the reverse), or red for a reason other than the one it names = Major; a new row no mutant turns red and that is not a declared control = Major; a row that depends on the machine (culture, zone, clock, port, environment variable, writable path outside the temp area, test order, an unseeded random) or leaves a directory behind = Minor (Major if it can fail on a Linux runner); a name lost, or a name added beyond the 18 = Major.
- **G-4 words and docs.** Any new or changed string in `src` that a calculator user or a staff reader can see = **Major** (the Warning is ruled unchanged; test names, test text and code comments are outside the words). A docs sentence in the branch that is not in the builder's WORDS-DRAFT (byte-equal, at the stated line) = **Major**; a WORDS-DRAFT row whose text or line disagrees with the head = Minor. A docs sentence or number that the importer code or the doc's own counts CONTRADICT = **Major**; a docs claim about a real book that nothing in the repo, the records or a synthetic run can prove (the builder's repeated-care-SKU sentence is the candidate) = **Minor** (named, with what a seat allowed to open the store would count). A docs number that is not in the table, the doc header or a cited record = Major (invented). The WORDS-DRAFT strings are pending the product owner's words: that fact is not a finding, and the gate never approves or judges wording.
- **G-5 confidentiality.** A real price, BID value, SKU, description or any value read from a real book (or from the real catalogue map) in an added line, a commit message, your STATUS or your evidence = **Blocker**; a decimal literal in a price shape that the builder did not declare synthetic where it is declared = Minor (Major if it equals a value you can show comes from a book). Synthetic constants must be declared synthetic.
- **G-6 CI.** A failing check-run on the head or the merge result = **Major**; a check `in_progress` at the verdict: the verdict line says `CI pending: <names>` and is at most GO WITH FINDINGS; a missing analysis is NOT scanned, never clean; a new open alert on the PR ref = **Blocker**.
- **G-7 hygiene.** A person's name, a secret, a customer datum in an added line or message = **Blocker**. A ticket key, a decision-card id, a gate finding id or a percent figure in a code line, test name or commit message = Major; **the brief number `B560` inside the new test file's NAME and in two commit-message lines is graded Minor** (the commission itself suggested the file name; the messages cannot be amended; the squash message is Tuesday's), and the keys `MPSCALC-147` and `-140` on docs lines that already carried them are listed, not findings. Non-ASCII bytes in added lines = Major. A build warning = Major.
- **G-8 delta and claims.** Always **Blocker**: a path beyond the 7; a byte changed in `SourceDocumentChecks.cs`, `ReferenceDocumentValidator.cs`, `.github/`, `web/`, a `.csproj`; the head moved. Always **Major**: a builder claim your measurement contradicts (7 commits, 7 paths, +512 -7, Engine 394 / Import 179 / Api 2,210 on the head's tree, 18 names added, 19 mutants, differential 55 cases, 0 warnings); a shared path with PR #102 or a live lane. **Always Note:** the squash artefact, CI pending at the verdict (stated), the builder's process disclosures (the stale heavy slot, a first mutant that was broken by its own delimiter and rebuilt, the rename commit), style.
- **G-9 compile and meaning coupling with PR #102** (C10): the head's new tests fail to compile or go red on the combination of #102's head and these 7 paths = **Major for the merge order** (Tuesday's call), recorded as such, never a finding against the builder's lines; not combined at the verdict = NOT TESTED, stated.

## LOAD (binding; the MPS heavy-slot convention, RELAYED from B538, B547 and B560)
8 cores. Heavy = `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, `dotnet test --list-tests`, `npm ci`, `npm test`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build`, a mutant matrix, a corpus replay at two trees, the full CI line. **Start a heavy run only while `uptime` load1 is below 7** at two reads 4 s apart (not independent: say so), recorded beside the result; at 7 or over poll every 20 s, log each wait, and do the light work meanwhile (C1, C2 reading, C7, C9 by GitHub, the oracle and corpus design, the Unicode generator, the docs scan). **Then take ONE slot atomically:** `mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-1' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-2' 2>/dev/null || mkdir '<project root>/.tools/heavy-slot-3'`, write `B562 <start time> <label>` into its `holder`; read every other holder first (measured at 07:49: slot 1 held by the stale B560 holder, slot 2 by B561; a holder that looks stale is reported, never removed or moved). All taken: wait 3 minutes and retry. At most ONE slot, one heavy job at a time; **re-read load1 at the start of EACH job inside a held slot**; at the run's end release ONLY your own slot, by the method the harness allows (if `rm` of your own holder is refused, as the builder met, say so and do not work around it: name it under Processes, slots, trees left). The shared `.tools/.git-lock` wraps GIT REF WRITES ONLY (`fetch`, `worktree add`). Write your OWN wrapper in `.tools/b562/`. Never kill a process by name. **A wait loop never uses `pgrep -f` or `ps ... | grep` on a pattern that appears in its own command line (it matches itself and never ends: B553 ADDENDUM-1): wait on a PID you recorded with `kill -0`, on `wait $!`, or on a done-marker file the job writes.** NO artificial load. The SDK is project-local `<project root>/.tools/dotnet`, run with `-nodeReuse:false -p:UseSharedCompilation=false` and `DOTNET_CLI_USE_MSBUILD_SERVER=0`; record `node --version` (drafter: v26.8.1), `npm --version` (11.19.0) and `python3 --version`. CI uses node 24; the web tree is byte-equal to main (0 `web/` paths): run the web line once, if at all, and say it proves nothing beyond the PR's own web check.

